[
 {:name "mcp", :kind "module", :text "`mcp` gives each MCP server as a name and each tool of the server as a function. `await mcp.<server>.<tool>(**arguments)` calls the tool with keyword arguments, or with one dict. The answer is that of `mcp_call`: {server, tool, content, is_error}. The text of a content block is at content[i][\"text\"].\n\nWrite a server name or a tool name as an identifier: each character that an identifier cannot have becomes `_`. So `mcp.figma_dev.get_code` calls the tool `get-code` of the server `figma-dev`. `mcp[\"figma-dev\"][\"get-code\"]` takes the exact names.\n\n`doc(mcp.<server>.<tool>)` prints the description and the input schema of one tool. `doc(mcp.<server>)` prints the tools and the prompts of the server. `doc` connects the server when that is necessary. `await mcp.<server>()` gives the tools with their input schemas as data. `apropos(pattern)` finds the tools and the prompts of the connected servers.\n\nPrompts: `doc(mcp.<server>.prompts.<name>)` prints one prompt, with its text when the prompt needs no argument. `await mcp.<server>.prompts.<name>(**arguments)` gets its messages, and `await mcp.<server>.prompts()` lists the prompts.\n\nResources are attachments. `list_attachments()` also lists the resources of the connected servers, each with `source` \"mcp\". `get_attachment`, `read_attachment` and `show_attachment` take the `id` of such a row, or the URI of the resource.\n\nThe servers and their status are in `session[\"env\"][\"mcp\"][\"servers\"]`. `mcp_call(server, tool, args)` is the same call with the names as strings."}]
